  <section class="hero" style="border-top:none;">
    <span class="eyebrow">Alpha · self-hosted · open source</span>
    <h1>Manage Docker like a <span>pilot</span>, not a passenger</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      ComposeControl is a C# and Blazor console for Docker and Docker Compose — the approachable stack workflow
      of Dockge, a built-in app catalog for one-click self-hosting, and progressively deeper environment
      management inspired by Portainer. One container, plain Compose files on disk, no vendor lock-in.
    </p>
    <div class="badges">
      <span class="badge">.NET 9</span>
      <span class="badge">Blazor Server</span>
      <span class="badge">Docker CLI-native</span>
      <span class="badge">Multi-host agents</span>
      <span class="badge">Status: alpha</span>
    </div>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#quick-start">Quick start →</a>
      <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="https://github.com/stoxello/ComposeControl" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View source on GitHub</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="overview" class="anchor-offset">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">How it works</span>
      <h2>No SDK, no hidden state</h2>
      <p>ComposeControl is an ASP.NET Core / Blazor (Interactive Server) app. Instead of a Docker SDK, it shells
      out to the <code>docker</code> and <code>docker compose</code> CLIs and parses their JSON output — the
      same thing you'd run by hand. Every stack it creates is written as an ordinary <code>compose.yaml</code>
      (and optional <code>.env</code>) under the stacks directory, so it stays fully inspectable and portable.
      Managed stacks are sandboxed by name to that directory, and only stacks ComposeControl created can be edited
      or rewritten from the UI.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="features" class="anchor-offset">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">What it does</span>
      <h2>Features</h2>
      <p>ComposeControl covers the full loop from discovering what's running to deploying, editing, backing up,
      and replicating it — on your local Docker host or across a fleet of remote agents.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="feature-group-title">Workspace</div>
    <div class="grid cols-3">
      <div class="card"><div class="ico">✦</div><h3>App catalog</h3><p>A searchable, built-in catalog of popular self-hosted apps with parameterized Compose previews and one-click deploy into an isolated stack directory.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="ico">▤</div><h3>Stacks</h3><p>Discovers every Compose project on the host, including ComposeControl-managed stacks whose deploy never completed. Create, edit, and redeploy stacks from an in-app editor.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="ico">↻</div><h3>Lifecycle actions</h3><p>Start, stop, restart, pull updates, and remove a stack. Removing a managed stack also deletes its files from disk.</p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="feature-group-title">Resources</div>
    <div class="grid cols-3">
      <div class="card"><div class="ico">▣</div><h3>Containers</h3><p>Discovery and filtering, plus a detail view with logs, live CPU / memory / disk charts, and an interactive in-browser shell.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="ico">◆</div><h3>Images</h3><p>List every local image with in-use detection, so you know what's safe to prune.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="ico">◎</div><h3>Networks</h3><p>List with in-use detection; create and remove custom Docker networks.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="ico">⬡</div><h3>Volumes</h3><p>List with in-use detection; create and remove volumes.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="ico">⌂</div><h3>Volume browser</h3><p>Browse a volume's file tree and edit text files in place — without shelling into a container.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="ico">≋</div><h3>Events &amp; audit log</h3><p>A live Docker engine event feed alongside a ComposeControl audit log of deploys, removals, edits, and sign-ins.</p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="feature-group-title">Resilience</div>
    <div class="grid cols-2">
      <div class="card"><div class="ico">B</div><h3>Backup</h3><p>Scheduled full and incremental backups of a stack's volumes and Compose configuration to the local host, with configurable retention and an option to stop the stack for a consistent snapshot.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="ico">R</div><h3>Replication</h3><p>Copy a stack to a registered ComposeControl agent and keep a cold replica in sync on a schedule, ready for failover, with optional bandwidth capping.</p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="feature-group-title">Administration</div>
    <div class="grid cols-3">
      <div class="card"><div class="ico">●</div><h3>Environments / Agents</h3><p>Register remote ComposeControl Agent instances and switch the Control Center between Docker hosts from a single top-bar selector.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="ico">⇪</div><h3>Update checks</h3><p>ComposeControl compares its running image digest against the published one and flags the sidebar when a newer version is available.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="ico">⚿</div><h3>Registry credentials</h3><p>Sign Docker in to a private registry (GHCR, Docker Hub, or custom) on the local host or a specific agent, so private images pull cleanly during installs and updates.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="ico">⚙</div><h3>Authentication</h3><p>ASP.NET Core Identity login with account management and two-factor authentication. A one-time first-run wizard creates the initial administrator; public registration stays disabled.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="ico">▦</div><h3>Engine overview</h3><p>Host name, Docker version, OS, and container / image counts on the dashboard, with graceful handling when the engine is offline.</p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <h4>Not yet built</h4>
      <p style="margin:0;color:var(--text-dim);">Per-container lifecycle actions, role-based access control for multiple users, remote shell sessions to agent hosts, and one-click self-update are on the <a href="#roadmap">roadmap</a>. Stack file editing and remote shell currently run on the Control Center host only.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

