  <section id="quick-start" class="anchor-offset">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Install</span>
      <h2>Quick start with Docker</h2>
      <p>This is the recommended way to run ComposeControl. It pulls the published image — no build step required.</p>
    </div>

    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <h4>Check prerequisites</h4>
        <p>Docker Engine with the Compose v2 plugin (<code>docker compose</code>). On Windows/macOS, Docker Desktop covers this.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h4>Get the Compose file</h4>
        <p>Clone the repo, or create a <code>compose.yaml</code> yourself:</p>
<pre><code>git clone https://github.com/stoxello/ComposeControl.git
cd ComposeControl</code></pre>
<pre><code>services:
  ComposeControl:
    image: ghcr.io/stoxello/ComposeControl:latest
    pull_policy: always
    container_name: ComposeControl
    restart: unless-stopped
    ports:
      - "${ComposeControl_PORT:-8080}:8080"
    volumes:
      - /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock
      - ./data:/app/data
      - ./keys:/app/keys
      - ./stacks:/opt/stacks</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h4>Start it</h4>
<pre><code>docker compose up -d</code></pre>
        <p>To use a different host port, set <code>ComposeControl_PORT</code> (for example in a <code>.env</code> file next to <code>compose.yaml</code>): <code>ComposeControl_PORT=9000</code>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h4>Sign in</h4>
        <p>Open <strong>http://localhost:8080</strong>, create the initial administrator account, and sign in. Public registration is disabled after that — only this first-run account can be created without authentication.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="callout">
      <h4>Private GHCR package?</h4>
      <p style="margin:0;color:var(--text-dim);">Run <code>docker login ghcr.io</code> once before <code>docker compose up -d</code>, or add the credential through ComposeControl's <a href="#registries">registry credentials</a> page after first sign-in.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="agents" class="anchor-offset">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Multi-host</span>
      <h2>Remote agents</h2>
      <p>Install <code>ComposeControl.Agent</code> on another Docker host when you want one ComposeControl Control Center
      to manage multiple engines. The agent is a small, token-protected HTTP API that runs Docker CLI commands
      against the host it's installed on — it never talks back to the Control Center on its own.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <h3 style="margin-top:0;">Deploy the agent</h3>
        <ol class="steps">
          <li>
            <h4>On the remote Docker host</h4>
<pre><code>curl -O https://raw.githubusercontent.com/stoxello/ComposeControl/main/compose.agent.yaml
printf "ComposeControl_AGENT_TOKEN=$(openssl rand -hex 32)\n" &gt; .env
docker compose -f compose.agent.yaml up -d</code></pre>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h4>Register it in ComposeControl</h4>
            <p>Open ComposeControl → <strong>Environments</strong> → <em>Connect a Docker host</em>, and add:</p>
            <ul style="color:var(--text-dim);margin:0 0 0 18px;padding:0;">
              <li><strong>Name</strong> — a friendly label for the host</li>
              <li><strong>Endpoint</strong> — <code>http://&lt;remote-host&gt;:5151</code></li>
              <li><strong>Access token</strong> — the <code>ComposeControl_AGENT_TOKEN</code> value from the remote host's <code>.env</code></li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h4>Switch environments</h4>
            <p>Use the top-bar environment selector to switch between <em>This host</em> and registered agents. Containers, Stacks, Images, Networks, Volumes, and Events follow the selected environment; stack creation and app catalog installs deploy to it too.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="card" style="margin-bottom:16px;">
          <h3 style="margin-top:0;">Stacks created for an agent</h3>
          <p>Stored under <code>/opt/ComposeControl/stacks</code> on that host by default. Set <code>ComposeControl_AGENT_STACKS_ROOT</code> in the agent's <code>.env</code> to use a different absolute host path.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3 style="margin-top:0;">What the agent exposes</h3>
          <p style="margin-bottom:12px;">A Bearer-token-authenticated HTTP API the Control Center calls on your behalf:</p>
          <ul class="api-list">
            <li><span class="method">GET</span> <code>/health</code></li>
            <li><span class="method">POST</span> <code>/api/stacks/deploy</code></li>
            <li><span class="method">POST</span> <code>/api/stacks/files</code> / <code>/save</code></li>
            <li><span class="method">POST</span> <code>/api/docker/commands</code></li>
            <li><span class="method">POST</span> <code>/api/registry/login</code> / <code>/logout</code></li>
            <li><span class="method">POST</span> <code>/api/replication/files</code></li>
            <li><span class="method">POST</span> <code>/api/replication/volumes/import</code> / <code>/export</code></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="alert">
      <span class="ico">⚠</span>
      <div>
        <strong>Troubleshooting a restarting agent</strong>
        Check that <code>.env</code> sits next to <code>compose.agent.yaml</code> and contains a non-empty <code>ComposeControl_AGENT_TOKEN</code> — a missing token makes the agent exit on startup.
<pre style="margin-top:10px;"><code>docker compose -f compose.agent.yaml config
docker logs --tail 100 ComposeControl-agent</code></pre>
        <p style="margin:10px 0 0;">If logs show <code>exec /usr/bin/dotnet: input/output error</code>, verify host Docker storage: <code>docker run --rm mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspnet:9.0 dotnet --info</code>. If that also fails, run <a href="https://github.com/stoxello/ComposeControl/blob/main/scripts/repair-docker-dotnet-io.sh" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><code>scripts/repair-docker-dotnet-io.sh</code></a> on the Ubuntu host — normally first, and only with <code>--reset-docker-storage</code> if Docker still can't execute the base image.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="resilience" class="anchor-offset">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Stack resilience</span>
      <h2>Backup &amp; replication</h2>
      <p>Both features run from a scheduled plan you create once; ComposeControl then runs it in the background.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="card">
        <h3 style="margin-top:0;">Backup — <span style="color:var(--text-dim);font-weight:400;">local snapshots</span></h3>
        <p>Go to <strong>Backup</strong>, pick a source stack, and choose a schedule (every 15 min up to daily). Set
        how often a full backup runs versus an incremental one, and how many full backup chains to retain — older
        chains are pruned automatically after a new full backup completes.</p>
        <div class="pill-row">
          <span class="pill">Volumes</span>
          <span class="pill">Record images</span>
          <span class="pill">Stop stack for snapshot</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3 style="margin-top:0;">Replication — <span style="color:var(--text-dim);font-weight:400;">cold standby</span></h3>
        <p>Go to <strong>Replication</strong>, pick a source stack and a registered target agent, and choose a
        schedule. Volumes are streamed to the target as a tar archive over the agent API; a bandwidth cap can be
        stored with the job.</p>
        <div class="pill-row">
          <span class="pill">Images</span>
          <span class="pill">Volumes</span>
          <span class="pill">Stop source for snapshot</span>
          <span class="pill">Bandwidth cap</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="configuration" class="anchor-offset">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Reference</span>
      <h2>Configuration</h2>
      <p>ComposeControl is configured through environment variables. The defaults below are what the container image ships with.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Default (container)</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>ComposeControl_PORT</code></td><td><code>8080</code></td><td>Host port mapped to the container, set in the Compose <code>.env</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ASPNETCORE_URLS</code></td><td><code>http://+:8080</code></td><td>Address the app listens on inside the container</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ConnectionStrings__DefaultConnection</code></td><td><code>DataSource=/app/data/ComposeControl.db</code></td><td>SQLite connection string for Identity and app data</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ComposeControl__DataPath</code></td><td><code>/app/data</code></td><td>Persistent app state directory</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ComposeControl__StacksPath</code></td><td><code>/opt/stacks</code></td><td>Directory where ComposeControl writes the Compose stacks it manages</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ComposeControl__DataProtectionPath</code></td><td><code>/app/keys</code></td><td>Where ASP.NET Core data-protection keys are persisted</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ComposeControl__AgentsPath</code></td><td><code>/app/data/agents.json</code></td><td>Registered remote ComposeControl agents</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ComposeControl__ReplicationPath</code></td><td><code>/app/data/replication.json</code></td><td>Replication plans and job history</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ComposeControl__ReplicaStacksPath</code></td><td><code>/app/data/replicas</code></td><td>Local cold-replica Compose files and volume archives</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p style="color:var(--text-dim);font-size:0.92rem;">Account policy: passwords must be at least 10 characters. Email confirmation is disabled (no SMTP configured), so accounts are usable immediately after creation.</p>

    <h3 style="margin-top:32px;">Persistent data</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-dim);">The Compose file mounts the Docker socket plus three writable directories. Keep <code>./data</code>, <code>./keys</code>, and <code>./stacks</code> to preserve state across upgrades.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Host path</th><th>Container path</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>/var/run/docker.sock</code></td><td><code>/var/run/docker.sock</code></td><td>Lets ComposeControl drive the host Docker engine</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>./data</code></td><td><code>/app/data</code></td><td>SQLite database, registered agents, replication plans, and local replica metadata</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>./keys</code></td><td><code>/app/keys</code></td><td>Data-protection keys — persisting these keeps logins valid across restarts</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>./stacks</code></td><td><code>/opt/stacks</code></td><td>Compose stacks created and managed by ComposeControl</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="alert danger" id="registries">
      <span class="ico">⚿</span>
      <div>
        <strong>Docker socket = host admin</strong>
        ComposeControl mounts <code>/var/run/docker.sock</code>. Access to this socket is equivalent to administrative control of the Docker host. Keep ComposeControl behind a reverse proxy that terminates HTTPS, never expose it directly to the public internet, and treat anyone who can reach the UI as a host administrator. Public registration stays disabled — only the first-run administrator can be created without authentication.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="updating" class="anchor-offset">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Stay current</span>
      <h2>Updating &amp; deploying</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <h3 style="margin-top:0;">Update the Control Center</h3>
        <p>ComposeControl's <strong>Updates</strong> page compares its running image digest against the published one and
        badges the sidebar when a newer version exists. From the directory containing your <code>compose.yaml</code>:</p>
<pre><code>docker compose pull
docker compose up -d</code></pre>
        <p style="color:var(--text-dim);font-size:0.92rem;">ComposeControl restarts on the new image. Your data, keys, and stacks are preserved by the mounted volumes. One-click self-update is on the <a href="#roadmap">roadmap</a> — today you run the two commands above yourself.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 style="margin-top:0;">Update an agent</h3>
        <p>Same idea, from the directory containing <code>compose.agent.yaml</code> on the remote host:</p>
<pre><code>docker compose -f compose.agent.yaml pull
docker compose -f compose.agent.yaml up -d</code></pre>
        <p style="color:var(--text-dim);font-size:0.92rem;">The image uses <code>pull_policy: always</code>, so a plain <code>up -d</code> after a fresh <code>pull</code> is enough to move to the latest published agent build.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3 id="deploying">Build the Control Center from source</h3>
    <p>To build the image locally instead of pulling from GHCR, layer the build overlay over the base Compose file:</p>
<pre><code>docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.build.yaml up -d --build</code></pre>
    <p style="color:var(--text-dim);">This builds from the repository <code>Dockerfile</code> — a multi-stage build on the .NET 9 SDK that also copies the Docker CLI and Compose plugin into the runtime image.</p>

    <h3>Local development</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-dim);">Requires the <a href="https://dotnet.microsoft.com/download" target="_blank" rel="noopener">.NET 9 SDK</a> and a running Docker Desktop or Docker Engine.</p>
<pre><code>dotnet run --project ./src/ComposeControl.Web</code></pre>
    <p style="color:var(--text-dim);">Open <strong>http://localhost:5146</strong> (or <code>https://localhost:7142</code>), create the initial account, and sign in. Docker must be running for engine data and deployments to work.</p>

    <h3>Run the test suite</h3>
<pre><code>dotnet test</code></pre>
    <p style="color:var(--text-dim);">The <code>tests/ComposeControl.Web.Tests</code> project covers the app catalog, app deployment, stack, setup, resource (image/network/volume), agent registry, backup, replication, registry credential, audit, and update services.</p>

    <h3>Publish the image (maintainers)</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-dim);">The public image is published to GitHub Container Registry as <code>ghcr.io/stoxello/ComposeControl</code>, using a GitHub Personal Access Token with the <code>write:packages</code> scope, supplied through an environment variable — never hard-coded or committed.</p>
<pre><code>$env:GHCR_TOKEN = "&lt;github-pat-with-write:packages&gt;"
.\ghcr-publish.ps1 -Owner stoxello -Version 0.1.0</code></pre>
    <p style="color:var(--text-dim);">The script logs in to <code>ghcr.io</code>, builds from the local <code>Dockerfile</code>, pushes the version tag, and also updates <code>:latest</code> (pass <code>-NoLatestTag</code> to skip that). It logs out afterward so credentials don't linger in your Docker config. The same script publishes the remote agent image:</p>
<pre><code>.\ghcr-publish.ps1 -Owner stoxello -Image ComposeControl-agent -Version 0.1.0</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section id="structure" class="anchor-offset">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Under the hood</span>
      <h2>Project structure</h2>
    </div>
<pre><code>src/ComposeControl.Web        Blazor UI, Identity, app catalog, and Docker services
  Components/             Razor pages and layouts (Apps, Stacks, Containers, Account)
  Services/                DockerCliService, StackService, AppDeploymentService, SetupService,
                            BackupService, ReplicationService, AgentRegistry, UpdateService
  Data/                    EF Core DbContext and Identity migrations
src/ComposeControl.Agent       Lightweight token-protected HTTP agent for remote Docker hosts
tests/ComposeControl.Web.Tests xUnit test suite
Dockerfile                Multi-stage build (SDK build -&gt; aspnet runtime + docker CLI)
compose.agent.yaml        Deployment Compose file for a remote ComposeControl Agent
compose.yaml               Deployment Compose file (pulls from GHCR)
compose.build.yaml        Overlay to build the image from source
ghcr-publish.ps1           Build and publish images to GitHub Container Registry</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section id="roadmap" class="anchor-offset">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">What's next</span>
      <h2>Roadmap</h2>
    </div>
    <ul style="color:var(--text-dim);padding-left:20px;">
      <li>Per-container lifecycle actions (start, stop, restart, remove)</li>
